    "what_it_is": "Heptanal is a medium-chain fatty aldehyde, a type of molecule naturally produced in the body when cell membrane lipids break down. In daily life, you might encounter its sharp, fruity, or slightly fatty aroma in various foods, teas, and essential oils. Because it is an endogenous compound that shifts during certain metabolic states, it has caught the attention of researchers as a potential biological signal.",
    "found_in": "You can find heptanal naturally occurring in everyday staples like oats, corn, and tea, as well as in botanicals like rooibos, cannabis, and mandarin oranges.",
    "why_researchers_care": "Researchers are fascinated by heptanal because it acts as a natural biomarker in the human body, particularly in how it fluctuates during oxidative stress and cell membrane changes. Much of the excitement centers on its potential as a diagnostic tool, as it has been detected at altered levels in the blood of lung cancer patients. The strongest evidence exploring its roles comes from human observational studies (Oxford level 2b), which are helping scientists map out how this simple aldehyde interacts with our metabolism and immune system.",
